DIHEDRAL GROUPS, POLYGONS, AND THE RAPIDITY-ALKANE CORRESPONDENCE
======================================================================

======================================================================
I. THE HYDROGEN COUNT IS THE DIHEDRAL ORDER
======================================================================

  Alkane C_n H_{2n+2}: hydrogen count = 2n+2 = 2(n+1).
  Dihedral group D_{n+1}: order = 2(n+1).

  n   Alkane    H count   D_{n+1}   |D_{n+1}|   Musical interval
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
  1   C1H4         4      D_2          4        2  octave
  2   C2H6         6      D_3          6        3/2  fifth
  3   C3H8         8      D_4          8        4/3  fourth
  4   C4H10       10      D_5         10        5/4  maj 3rd
  5   C5H12       12      D_6         12        6/5  min 3rd
  6   C6H14       14      D_7         14        7/6  septimal
  7   C7H16       16      D_8         16        8/7  maj 2nd
  8   C8H18       18      D_9         18        9/8  pyth 2nd
  9   C9H20       20      D_10        20        10/9  

  METHANE CH4 has 4 hydrogens = |D_2| = Klein four-group.
  ETHANE C2H6 has 6 hydrogens = |D_3| = symmetry group of TRIANGLE.
  PROPANE C3H8 has 8 hydrogens = |D_4| = symmetry group of SQUARE.
  BUTANE C4H10 has 10 hydrogens = |D_5| = symmetry group of PENTAGON.
  PENTANE C5H12 has 12 hydrogens = |D_6| = symmetry group of HEXAGON.

  The n-th alkane's hydrogen count is the order of the
  symmetry group of the (n+1)-gon!

  And the musical interval (n+1)/n = Q(1/(2n+1)) uses
  the SAME number n+1 as the polygon vertex count.

======================================================================
II. REGULAR POLYGONS ON THE UNIT CIRCLE
======================================================================

  The (n+1)-gon has vertices at omega_k = e^{2*pi*i*k/(n+1)},
  k = 0, 1, ..., n.

  Apply the Cayley transform Q(z) = (1+z)/(1-z) to each vertex:

  2-GON (alkane C1H4, interval 2/1):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = -1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+0.0000i  |Q| = 0.0000

  3-GON (alkane C2H6, interval 3/2):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = -0.500+0.866i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+0.5774i  |Q| = 0.5774
    k=2: z = -0.500-0.866i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000-0.5774i  |Q| = 0.5774

  4-GON (alkane C3H8, interval 4/3):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = +0.000+1.000i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+1.0000i  |Q| = 1.0000
    k=2: z = -1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+0.0000i  |Q| = 0.0000
    k=3: z = -0.000-1.000i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-1.0000i  |Q| = 1.0000

  5-GON (alkane C4H10, interval 5/4):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = +0.309+0.951i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+1.3764i  |Q| = 1.3764
    k=2: z = -0.809+0.588i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+0.3249i  |Q| = 0.3249
    k=3: z = -0.809-0.588i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-0.3249i  |Q| = 0.3249
    k=4: z = +0.309-0.951i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-1.3764i  |Q| = 1.3764

  6-GON (alkane C5H12, interval 6/5):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = +0.500+0.866i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+1.7321i  |Q| = 1.7321
    k=2: z = -0.500+0.866i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+0.5774i  |Q| = 0.5774
    k=3: z = -1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+0.0000i  |Q| = 0.0000
    k=4: z = -0.500-0.866i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000-0.5774i  |Q| = 0.5774
    k=5: z = +0.500-0.866i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000-1.7321i  |Q| = 1.7321

  7-GON (alkane C6H14, interval 7/6):
    k=0: z = 1.000+0.000i  =>  Q(z) = INFINITY (pole)
    k=1: z = +0.623+0.782i  =>  Q(z) = -0.0000+2.0765i  |Q| = 2.0765
    k=2: z = -0.223+0.975i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+0.7975i  |Q| = 0.7975
    k=3: z = -0.901+0.434i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000+0.2282i  |Q| = 0.2282
    k=4: z = -0.901-0.434i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-0.2282i  |Q| = 0.2282
    k=5: z = -0.223-0.975i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-0.7975i  |Q| = 0.7975
    k=6: z = +0.623-0.782i  =>  Q(z) = +0.0000-2.0765i  |Q| = 2.0765

======================================================================
III. Q MAPS POLYGONS TO THE IMAGINARY AXIS
======================================================================

  For z = e^{i*theta} on the unit circle (z != 1):
  Q(z) = (1+z)/(1-z) = i * cot(theta/2)

  PROOF: z = e^{i*theta}
  1+z = 1+cos(t)+i*sin(t) = 2*cos^2(t/2) + 2i*sin(t/2)*cos(t/2)
      = 2*cos(t/2) * [cos(t/2) + i*sin(t/2)] = 2*cos(t/2)*e^{i*t/2}
  1-z = 1-cos(t)-i*sin(t) = 2*sin^2(t/2) - 2i*sin(t/2)*cos(t/2)
      = 2*sin(t/2) * [sin(t/2) - i*cos(t/2)]
      = -2i*sin(t/2) * [cos(t/2) + i*sin(t/2)] = -2i*sin(t/2)*e^{i*t/2}
  Q(z) = 2*cos(t/2)*e^{i*t/2} / (-2i*sin(t/2)*e^{i*t/2})
       = cos(t/2) / (-i*sin(t/2)) = i*cot(t/2). QED.

  So Q sends the unit circle to the IMAGINARY AXIS.
  The (n+1)-gon at angles theta_k = 2*pi*k/(n+1) maps to:
  Q(omega_k) = i * cot(pi*k/(n+1))  for k = 1, ..., n
  (k=0 gives the pole at z=1)

  The COTANGENT VALUES at polygon vertices:

  3-gon: cot(pi*k/3) for k=1,...,2
    k=1: cot(pi*1/3) = +0.577350  = 1/sqrt(3)
    k=2: cot(pi*2/3) = -0.577350

  4-gon: cot(pi*k/4) for k=1,...,3
    k=1: cot(pi*1/4) = +1.000000  = 1
    k=2: cot(pi*2/4) = +0.000000  = 0
    k=3: cot(pi*3/4) = -1.000000  = -1

  5-gon: cot(pi*k/5) for k=1,...,4
    k=1: cot(pi*1/5) = +1.376382
    k=2: cot(pi*2/5) = +0.324920
    k=3: cot(pi*3/5) = -0.324920
    k=4: cot(pi*4/5) = -1.376382

  6-gon: cot(pi*k/6) for k=1,...,5
    k=1: cot(pi*1/6) = +1.732051  = sqrt(3)
    k=2: cot(pi*2/6) = +0.577350  = 1/sqrt(3)
    k=3: cot(pi*3/6) = +0.000000  = 0
    k=4: cot(pi*4/6) = -0.577350
    k=5: cot(pi*5/6) = -1.732051

  7-gon: cot(pi*k/7) for k=1,...,6
    k=1: cot(pi*1/7) = +2.076521
    k=2: cot(pi*2/7) = +0.797473
    k=3: cot(pi*3/7) = +0.228243
    k=4: cot(pi*4/7) = -0.228243
    k=5: cot(pi*5/7) = -0.797473
    k=6: cot(pi*6/7) = -2.076521

  8-gon: cot(pi*k/8) for k=1,...,7
    k=1: cot(pi*1/8) = +2.414214
    k=2: cot(pi*2/8) = +1.000000  = 1
    k=3: cot(pi*3/8) = +0.414214
    k=4: cot(pi*4/8) = +0.000000  = 0
    k=5: cot(pi*5/8) = -0.414214
    k=6: cot(pi*6/8) = -1.000000  = -1
    k=7: cot(pi*7/8) = -2.414214

======================================================================
IV. THE PENTAGON AND THE GOLDEN RATIO
======================================================================

  The 5-gon (pentagon) has vertices at e^{2*pi*i*k/5}, k=0,...,4.
  cot(pi/5) = cot(36 deg) = sqrt(1 + 2/sqrt(5)) = ... 
  = 1.3763819205
  = sqrt(1 + 2/sqrt(5)) = 1.3763819205

  cot(pi/5) = 1.3763819205
  cot(2*pi/5) = 0.3249196962

  cot(pi/5)^2 = 1.8944271910
  5 + 2*sqrt(5) = 9.4721359550
  cos(2*pi/5) = 0.3090169944
  (sqrt(5)-1)/4 = 0.3090169944

  Pentagon maps under Q to:
  k=0: pole (infinity)
  k=1: i * cot(pi/5) = i * 1.376382
  k=2: i * cot(2*pi/5) = i * 0.324920
  k=3: i * cot(3*pi/5) = i * -0.324920
  k=4: i * cot(4*pi/5) = i * -1.376382

  cot(pi/5) * cot(2*pi/5) = 0.4472135955
  Compare: 1/sqrt(5) = 0.4472135955
  Product of all cot(pi*k/5): 0.2000000000
  = 1/5: 0.2000000000
  Match: True

  THEOREM: For odd prime p,
  prod_{k=1}^{p-1} cot(pi*k/p) = 1/p (up to sign).

  Verification:
    p= 3: product = -0.3333333333, 1/p = 0.3333333333, match: True
    p= 5: product = +0.2000000000, 1/p = 0.2000000000, match: True
    p= 7: product = -0.1428571429, 1/p = 0.1428571429, match: True
    p=11: product = -0.0909090909, 1/p = 0.0909090909, match: True
    p=13: product = +0.0769230769, 1/p = 0.0769230769, match: True

  So Q maps the p-gon to points on the imaginary axis whose
  cotangent product equals 1/p. This is a DEEP identity relating
  the Cayley transform to roots of unity.

======================================================================
V. THE DIHEDRAL GROUP ACTION IN RAPIDITY SPACE
======================================================================

  D_n acts on the n-gon by rotations and reflections.
  Rotation by 2*pi/n: omega_k -> omega_{k+1}.
  Reflection: omega_k -> omega_{-k} = conjugate(omega_k).

  Through Q, these become actions on the imaginary axis:
  Rotation: i*cot(pi*k/n) -> i*cot(pi*(k+1)/n)
  Reflection: i*cot(pi*k/n) -> i*cot(-pi*k/n) = -i*cot(pi*k/n)
  (conjugation becomes NEGATION on the imaginary axis!)

  So the dihedral reflection becomes SIGN FLIP in the Cayley image.
  This is the same as RAPIDITY INVERSION: phi -> -phi.
  Reflection = time reversal = rapidity negation.

======================================================================
VI. POLYGON DIAGONALS AND RAPIDITY INTERVALS
======================================================================

  In the n-gon, the diagonal from vertex 0 to vertex k
  has length |omega_0 - omega_k| = 2*sin(pi*k/n).
  The RAPIDITY of this length: ln(2*sin(pi*k/n))/2.

  5-GON diagonals:
    k=1: length = 2*sin(pi*1/5) = 1.175571, rapidity = 0.080877
    k=2: length = 2*sin(pi*2/5) = 1.902113, rapidity = 0.321483
    k=3: length = 2*sin(pi*3/5) = 1.902113, rapidity = 0.321483
  Diagonal ratios:
    d_2/d_1 = 1.618034 ~ phi!
    d_2/d_1 = 1.618034, rapidity = 0.240606
    d_3/d_2 = 1.000000, rapidity = 0.000000

  6-GON diagonals:
    k=1: length = 2*sin(pi*1/6) = 1.000000, rapidity = -0.000000
    k=2: length = 2*sin(pi*2/6) = 1.732051, rapidity = 0.274653
    k=3: length = 2*sin(pi*3/6) = 2.000000, rapidity = 0.346574
  Diagonal ratios:
    d_2/d_1 = 1.732051, rapidity = 0.274653
    d_3/d_2 = 1.154701, rapidity = 0.071921

  7-GON diagonals:
    k=1: length = 2*sin(pi*1/7) = 0.867767, rapidity = -0.070916
    k=2: length = 2*sin(pi*2/7) = 1.563663, rapidity = 0.223516
    k=3: length = 2*sin(pi*3/7) = 1.949856, rapidity = 0.333878
    k=4: length = 2*sin(pi*4/7) = 1.949856, rapidity = 0.333878
  Diagonal ratios:
    d_2/d_1 = 1.801938, rapidity = 0.294431
    d_3/d_2 = 1.246980 ~ maj 3rd!
    d_3/d_2 = 1.246980, rapidity = 0.110362
    d_4/d_3 = 1.000000, rapidity = 0.000000

  12-GON diagonals:
    k=1: length = 2*sin(pi*1/12) = 0.517638, rapidity = -0.329239
    k=2: length = 2*sin(pi*2/12) = 1.000000, rapidity = -0.000000
